    <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-ink/85 leading-relaxed space-y-6">
      <h2>About Starise</h2>
      <p>
        Starise is a venture capital fund focused on early-stage companies in
        <strong>artificial intelligence</strong>, <strong>clean energy</strong>,
        and <strong>fintech</strong>. The fund deploys capital across emerging
        markets — primarily Africa, South and Southeast Asia, and Latin America —
        regions where the next wave of growth meets significant unmet needs.
      </p>
      <p>
        Starise is backed by <strong>Transsion Holdings</strong>, the global
        mobile and IoT operator behind the Tecno, Itel, and Infinix brands —
        Transsion's devices ship to over 100 million users annually,
        predominantly across the same growth markets Starise invests in.
      </p>

      <h2>What we built</h2>
      <p>
        bbd.sh studio worked with the Starise team on the public-facing brand
        site at <a href="https://www.starise.io" target="_blank" class="text-electric hover:underline">starise.io</a>.
        The brief was simple: a landing presence that earns the brand's place
        in serious VC conversations, with technical foundations that age well.
      </p>

      <h3>Scope</h3>
      <ul>
        <li>Brand site architecture and information design</li>
        <li>Vite + React frontend, deployed as a static asset bundle</li>
        <li>Full SEO foundation: meta tags, OG / Twitter cards, JSON-LD schema (Organization, WebSite), canonical declarations, robots.txt, sitemap.xml</li>
        <li>Google Search Console verification + Bing Webmaster import</li>
        <li>IndexNow integration to surface new pages to Bing and Yandex within minutes of publish</li>
        <li>Client-deployed infrastructure (the team manages their own nginx / hosting)</li>
      </ul>

      <h3>Constraints we worked under</h3>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Brand collision</strong>: "Starise" overlaps with several pre-existing entities — a Japanese tower / studio company, a Vocaloid song, a few minor commercial vendors. Search-engine entity disambiguation is a long game; we set the on-site fundamentals correctly so authority can compound over time.</li>
        <li><strong>Stealth-friendly initial launch</strong>: Starise's portfolio and team details are still being announced publicly, so the homepage stays high-level by design.</li>
        <li><strong>Client infrastructure</strong>: the client runs their own deploy pipeline — we shipped a clean dist bundle and a one-line deploy script.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Technology</h2>
      <p>
        Vite + React for the frontend (no framework lock-in beyond standard
        tooling). All SEO signals baked into the static HTML at build time —
        no JavaScript dependency for indexability. Hosted on the client's
        nginx + standard Linux stack.
      </p>

      <h2>What's next</h2>
      <p>
        The site is live and indexed. Near-term roadmap items include:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>Pre-rendered route content for /career and /news (so they're indexed independently from the homepage)</li>
        <li>Editorial cadence for /news — investment announcements, thesis posts, portfolio company features</li>
        <li>Off-site entity work — Crunchbase, LinkedIn, and partner network pages — so search engines confidently associate <strong>Starise</strong> with <strong>Transsion</strong> and the fund's actual scope</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Why this matters</h2>
      <p>
        For a fund whose portfolio companies will themselves need to be found
        by founders, journalists, and LPs — Starise's own discoverability
        sets the tone. Getting the SEO foundation right at launch is cheaper
        than rebuilding it after the fund has visibility but no authority.
      </p>

      <p>
